{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The trusted run captured the full order: term-2 replies were held, node `c` became leader in term 3 and completed the command at index 5, then the old node’s verification returned success while its FSM lacked that command. This supports the scoped authority-verification claim. The review will retain the timeout-reload settings, transport adaptation, and unresolved heartbeat-versus-append callback as limits."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > build_freshness_review.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nss=['s-verify-api','s-verify-documentation','s-leader-loop','s-verify-vote','s-replication','s-heartbeat','s-match-notify','s-rpc','s-transfer','s-vote','s-campaign','s-bootstrap','s-startup','s-fsm','s-test-fsm-check','s-public-reload-check','s-reloadable-fields-check','s-timeout-contract-check','s-config-timing-check','s-inmem-rpc-reload-check','s-inmem-link-check','s-transport-contract']\ndef item(aspect,rationale,limits):return dict(aspect=aspect,status='no_issue_found',source_ids=ss,rationale=rationale,counterevidence=[],limitations=limits)\nitems=[item('applicability','s-verify-api promises to ensure the peer is still leader and describes preventing stale FSM reads; s-verify-documentation assigns confirmation to voter heartbeats. The proposition uses a stronger independently measured antecedent than mere disconnection: another voter is elected at a strictly higher term and completes a command before the old VerifyLeader call. No point inside that later invocation can precede the completed transition. This supports the necessary condition that the old verification cannot legitimately assert current authority solely from older replies. Three fixed voters remove the separate nonvoter issue. Timeout configuration is explicitly per-server in s-timeout-contract-check, and s-reloadable-fields-check/s-public-reload-check permit changing these timing fields; all supplied values satisfy s-config-timing-check. Membership and protocol version remain unchanged. Acceptance by validation alone is not the legal-prefix argument: actual elections and committed Apply plus persisted membership and locked FSM checks establish it. Scope exclusion of configuration changes concerns the fixed participant configuration, while the plan expressly declares timeout reload. No acquired source states cross-node timeout equality or a read lease that excuses successful stale verification.', ['Executed timing values differ between peers and include public reload; equal-timeout deployment behavior is not established.','The conclusion is an implementation authority-verification violation with an observed missing command, not divergent committed history or a general linearizability theorem.']),item('decomposition','The producer creates an old-context success before the new election; the consumer later drains whatever futures are registered when notifyAll runs (s-replication/s-match-notify). No request-start or reply-production epoch accompanies the Boolean vote (s-verify-vote). The normal append comparison resp.Term > req.Term cannot reject this old but internally matching pair; heartbeat also consumes resp.Success. A fresh request after the transition would instead be rejected by s-rpc. Lease expiry, higher-term replies and negative notification remain protective alternatives, but all protocol workers and the lease timer stay active while the legal transport schedule withholds the relevant messages. On this execution successful verification precedes lease expiry. Each fixed identity has actual reply production, capture, context transition, post-transition admission and public result in one history. The earlier eligibility witness is independent: every participant here is a voter. The older node FSM lacks the already completed new command after verification success, measured under its mutex.', ['The first notification-producing path (heartbeat or ordinary append) is not isolated; both share the scoped delayed-support interpretation.','The condition is necessary but not sufficient for every valid verification; membership changes, other stale-request mechanisms and read application ordering remain outside this comparison.']),item('checker_correspondence','Read the fixed v1 harness and plan in direct-checks/adb25a646ba74d49b3c36b835a6c4601 and the complete output of execution a00213ac48c64ad09b67140a5fa792fe. Fresh BootstrapCluster/NewRaft instances generate the old election and committed prefix. Persisted configuration index 1 is decoded and compared with the public membership; locked MockFSM checks establish actual prefix application on all three nodes. The wrapper reserves the first post-activation call of each of two old sender loops per peer. It invokes real InmemTransport.AppendEntries, records actual request/response term, Success and error, and withholds return. Four captured matching positive replies must exist before link split and reload. No later old call reaches a receiver: subsequent calls park, and incoming links to old are disconnected. New node must be observed Leader with term > oldTerm; its Apply.Error must return nil and locked FSM must contain the new command before observe() invokes old.VerifyLeader. Fields superseded and target_id are derived independently from the selected node versus the actual new leader, not from output success. Public future.Error supplies success; no synthetic expected result is emitted. The transition prerequisite precedes admission by new_term reference, admission_alias explicitly selects admission, and op is independent operation identity. The implication superseded => \"'!success does not require success or fail a permitted pending control. Actual events 0/1/2 show all four held replies at term 2, new leader c at term 3, completed write index 5, and then old verification success=true while target_term=2, target_state=Leader and command_present=false. Events 3/4/5 identify the separate current-leader control in this same cluster; it succeeds at term 3 with command_present=true and antecedent false. Raw logs also contain the real term-3 election and earlier refused pre-vote, so the history is not fabricated from a constructor. Runner parsing is clean, both results correlate and no result is missing. The wrapper disables pipelines only on old transport; the newer leader uses the included in-memory pipeline to its remaining connected voter, visible in raw logs. That is compatible with the plan control: new-leader support is not being substituted or delayed. Cleanup releases parked workers and shuts down independently of a forbidden success; post-result link errors cannot explain away the prior result. No_issue_found is provisional scoped review of this fixed artifact and measured comparison.'\"', ['The response delay occurs after InmemTransport has produced a response but before it returns to the caller; actual TCP packet scheduling is not executed.','The 200 ms inner transport timeout does not bound the outer wrapper delay, which is an explicitly declared controlled scheduling adaptation; no protocol reply fields are altered.','The command-presence diagnostic is an observed endpoint, while only the authority-success implication is the formal property.'])]\ns=dict(action='review',artifact_id='fe886cb3d1954069a302fe565f96682c',rationale='The fixed run establishes the same-history higher-term commit before old verification and its public success using held old-context replies. Review the scoped contract, active protections, timing choices and actual predicate without generalizing to log safety.',review_items=items,feedback=dict(ref_ids=['fe886cb3d1954069a302fe565f96682c','a00213ac48c64ad09b67140a5fa792fe','s-heartbeat','s-replication','s-verify-vote','s-public-reload-check','s-timeout-contract-check'],answered='The same-history gaps are answered: real term-2 replies preceded real term-3 election, successful command at index 5 preceded old verification invocation, and that future returned success at term 2 with the new command absent from old FSM. The current-leader control completed independently. This decides the scoped stale-authority verification proposition without changing the earlier nonvoter obligation.',remaining=['The exact first callback supplying old verification remains unisolated.','Equal-timeout configuration and real TCP delay variants are outside this witness.'],understanding='updated',rationale='Existing map already records the producer/notification-consumer path; the measured temporal witness belongs in its CheckRun, not a universal Fact. Return to frontier: a same-timeout variant could broaden this result but is not required for its current proposition; pipeline error-to-unsafe-support remains ungrounded beyond interface misuse; persistent vote term/candidate writes are separate and source-grounded in b-vote and s-stable-context, with direct A2 election consequences. Prefer a bounded source investigation of vote persistence partial failure next: determine actual StableStore fault contract, error return paths and whether later duplicate-vote handling can reinterpret a partially written pair. Preserve current results and do not infer a new defect without that contract.',question_updates={'fd70a4f7b6a84307a7479cc396e5656c':dict(unknowns=['Which of the held heartbeat or ordinary append callbacks first supplied the verification is not isolated.','Scope remains the executed mixed-timeout, no-old-pipeline transport schedule; other timing/transport variants are not checked.'],resume_conditions=[])}))\njson.dump(s,open('freshness-review.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\npython3 build_freshness_review.py\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'));p=subprocess.run(r['validation']['command']+['freshness-review.json'],capture_output=True,text=True);print(p.stdout)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
